{"type":"thinking","text":"Both generators exist. Note: in compare_e2e_runs.py, there's a subtlety with the whitelist check: `not any(field.startswith(w + \".\") or w.startswith(field) for w in whitelist)`. For E-06 whitelist = {\"observed\"}. So any field under `observed.*` — field like `observed.first` startswith \"observed.\" → OK. And field \"observed\" itself → w.startswith(field) → \"observed\".startswith(\"observed\") → True → whitelisted. Good.\n\nWait — but the whitelist for E-06 is \"observed\" which allows ANY difference under observed.* to be whitelisted. That's broad but matches the claim (\"E-06 random reference fragments\"). Actually the R-03 requirement says: \"fix3 一致性 JSON 是否如实记录 observed 差异（E-06 随机段、E-07 计时）并以白名单口径定义 consistent=true，而非 differences: {}\". So the whitelist口径 is acceptable. But note: whitelist \"observed\" for E-06 is broader than \"随机段\" — it would whitelist any observed change in E-06 (e.g., status change is separate; but observed content like \"first\"/\"second\" replaced by something else). Minor. I'll note.\n\nHmm, actually a potential issue: for E-07 whitelist = {\"observed.p95_ms\", \"observed.timings_ms\"}; if `observed.timings_ms` is a list of different lengths, flatten gives the whole list as one field. Fine.\n\nAlso `field.startswith(w + \".\")` — for field \"observed.p95_ms\" and w \"observed.p95_ms\": startswith(\"observed.p95_ms.\") false; w.startswith(field) true. OK.\n\nNow read the fix3 consistency JSON and leakage JSON."}
