{"type":"thinking","text":"`.pytest_cache/v/cache/nodeids` at line 12391 — right after test_service.py (12382) and its pycache (12390). So the nodeids cache was written during the FIX-3 pytest run (after test_service.py was modified). ✓ This confirms the 43-nodeids cache reflects the FIX-3 verified tree, not a stale FIX-2 run. Strong cross-evidence for \"pytest 43 passed\" at the verified tree.\n\nWait, ordering: 12382 test_service.py, 12383-12389 FIX-3 code/docs, 12390 test_service pycache, 12391 .pytest_cache nodeids. Hmm, nodeids at 12391 > 12390 (pycache) → pytest ran after all FIX-3 source edits. ✓ And `.pytest_cache/v/cache/lastfailed` at 12326 — older than the FIX-3 run! Hmm: lastfailed is only rewritten when a test fails... Actually pytest writes lastfailed on each run (with the current failures; empty dict if none). Hmm, if it's empty and stale from an earlier run, that weakens the \"43 passed with no failures at FIX-3\" claim slightly: lastfailed might just be an old artifact (from a passing FIX-2 run at 12326).\n\nHmm, wait. Let me think about pytest cache behavior: `.pytest_cache/v/cache/lastfailed` is written at the end of each run (if `--lf` support enabled, which is default via cacheprovider). It writes the set of failed nodeids. If empty, it writes \"{}\". Actually, does pytest write the file when there are no failures? The cacheprovider's `pytest_sessionfinish` calls `self.cache.set(\"cache/lastfailed\", ...)` only if `self.cache` and... Let me recall: in `_pytest/cacheprovider.py`:\n\n```python\ndef pytest_sessionfinish(self, session):\n    config = self.config\n    if config.getoption(\"cacheshow\") or hasattr(config, \"workerinput\"):\n        return\n    assert config.cache is not None\n    saved_lastfailed = config.cache.get(\"cache/lastfailed\", {})\n    if saved_lastfailed != self.lastfailed:\n        config.cache.set(\"cache/lastfailed\", self.lastfailed)\n```\nSo it writes only when the value changes. If the FIX-2 run had failures... no, if